# the zimmermann telegram



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

on **march 1, 1917**, a secret message was made public.

it was called the **zimmermann telegram**.

this event made tensions between germany and the united states even worse.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

germany's foreign minister, **arthur zimmermann**, had sent this message to mexico.

he offered mexico a deal.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## what happened before that



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **1916**, there were many attacks along the border between america and mexico.

a man named **francisco "pancho" villa** was behind them.

he attacked towns in the united states.

the american army sent thousands of troops to the border.

their job: track down villa.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## germany's plan



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

germany saw a chance.

it wanted mexico to cause more trouble at the border if war broke out with the united states.

more attacks would mean more american troops stuck at the border.

those troops could not be sent to fight in europe.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in return, germany made a promise.

if germany won the war, it would give mexico back three regions: **texas**, **new mexico**, and **arizona**.

mexico had lost that land in **1848** after a war with the united states.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# why mexico could not join



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in reality, mexico could not take part in this plan.

even if it wanted to, it was too divided.

in **1917**, violent fighting raged among many groups inside mexico.

villa's forces were just one of them.

to understand why, we need to look back at how the trouble started.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the mexican revolution begins (1910 to 1913)



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **1910**, a liberal politician named **francisco madero** ran for president of mexico.

he challenged **porfirio díaz**.

díaz had held the presidency without a break since **1884**.

his government ruled like a dictatorship.

it favored wealthy landowners and business owners.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## the rigged election



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

díaz won the election.

the vote was rigged.

he then had madero arrested.

this act sparked uprisings across the country.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **1911**, díaz was forced to step down.

he left the country.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## madero's short rule



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

madero was declared president.

but he failed to deliver the changes people expected.

a new revolt broke out against his government.

in **1913**, madero was forced to give up his office.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

a general named **victoriano huerta** replaced him.

huerta commanded rebel forces within the mexican army.

soon after taking power, he had madero killed.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

huerta tried to rule like a dictator.

opposition rose quickly.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the factions fight (1913 to 1914)



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in the south, **emiliano zapata** led a rebellion.

his followers were indigenous farmers.

they wanted land reform.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in the north, three men led the revolution:

- **venustiano carranza**
- **álvaro obregón**
- **pancho villa**

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## america steps in



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

american president **woodrow wilson** backed carranza.

he supplied carranza's forces with weapons and supplies.

wilson also used this moment to order american marines into mexico.

his stated goal: protect property owned by americans.

in **april 1914**, the american navy took control of the mexican port of **veracruz**.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## huerta falls



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

throughout the summer of **1914**, the rebel groups fought each other.

each leader wanted to replace huerta.

in **july**, huerta fled into exile.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

carranza declared himself president.

villa and zapata objected.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# villa's raid and the response (1916)



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **march 1916**, villa attacked the town of **columbus** in new mexico.

this was payback for american support of carranza.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

wilson responded by sending **five thousand troops** across the border.

their mission: capture villa.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

villa escaped them.

within a year, the american troops had gone home.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## the fallout



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

this invasion damaged relations between the united states and carranza.

he refused to accept what he saw as an insult to mexico's right to rule itself.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

by **1917**, carranza was firmly in power.

but rebellion continued.

villa led fighters in the northern state of **chihuahua**.

zapata led fighters in **morelos** in the south.



&nbsp;
&nbsp;


# the united states reacts and enters the war



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

so when germany approached carranza with its offer of an alliance against the united states, he was not in a position to accept it.

he did not have the control or the stability to do so.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

but when the telegram became public, the united states was furious.

the country was not yet at war.

and germany was already planning how to break up its territory.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## money and business push toward war



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

american banks had lent money to the european allies.

if germany won, those loans would go unpaid.

so the banks supported american involvement.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

businesses saw a different benefit.

they hoped that if the united states helped the allies win, the allies would give them greater access to global markets.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;


## public anger grows



&nbsp;
&nbsp;

popular support for going to war against germany grew fast.

the country was already angry over the sinking of the **lusitania** and the **sussex**.

the zimmermann telegram added fuel to that fire.

&nbsp;
&nbsp;

in **april 1917**, the united states entered the conflict on the side of the allied powers.

&nbsp;


