# 1. support and opposition



many civilians fully supported the war effort.


others did not.


they voiced opposition to the war itself.


they also criticized the reasons behind it.


this disapproval grew stronger over time.


the war dragged on.


shortages continued.


morale on the home front suffered.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 2. food shortages in germany



in germany, soldiers came first in the food supply chain.


soldiers usually got enough to eat.


they may not have enjoyed the food.


but there was a steady amount of it.


civilians had a much harder time finding food.


the elderly were hit hardest.


people who did not work for the war effort also struggled.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

the british blockade made things worse.


it ran from 1914 until the end of the war.


bread rationing began in germany in 1915.


the winter of 1916 to 1917 was especially severe.


much of the potato crop was lost.


tens of thousands of people had nothing but turnips to eat.


food riots broke out from 1915 onward.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 3. food shortages in austria-hungary



austria-hungary also faced food shortages.


it did not have as much infrastructure as germany.


it had fewer options for transporting food.


the government focused on supplying soldiers first.


civilians came second.


disruptions to the food supply hurt morale at home.


some civilians began questioning whether the government was doing its job in fighting the war.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 4. growing civilian discontent



more young men were needed for the armies as the war went on.


the early excitement of 1914 disappeared.


some recruits lost confidence that their national army could win battles.


others did not believe it was worth risking their lives.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

some young men refused to fight on principle.


they had religious or philosophical reasons against fighting wars.


they did not wish to serve.


in the empires, some would-be soldiers questioned the value of fighting for imperial glory.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

canada faced a conscription crisis in 1917.


it had to use a draft to meet britain's demand for troops.


the military was called to stop a protest in quebec city.


soldiers fired on the protesters.


several people were killed.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 5. censorship and propaganda



censorship became a common tool to manage civilian discontent.


governments limited the information shared about the war effort.


newspapers gave only vague descriptions of battles and losses.


government-sponsored propaganda showed civilians pro-war posters and commentary.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

censorship efforts began in all combatant nations in 1914.


they expanded to the united states when it entered the war in 1917.


the united states government created the committee on public information.


it put out pro-war propaganda through public speeches and posters.


these efforts were remarkably effective.


most posters showed german troops as bloodthirsty monsters.


the messages warned that germans would cross the atlantic to attack the united states if they were not stopped.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 6. the espionage act and the sedition act



in 1917, the united states government moved to squash criticism of the war.


it passed the **espionage act**.


this law set limits on what could be mailed.


it banned any anti-war newspaper or pamphlet.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

in 1918, congress passed the **sedition act**.


this act forbade "disloyal, profane, scurrilous, and abusive" language.


the targets included:


- the decision to enter the war
- the draft
- the flag
- armed forces' uniforms



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

the law was not about what might be true.


it was about what could be seen as disloyal.


anti-war speech was considered disloyal.


disloyal speech was seditious.


people could not openly question why the united states was in the war.


they could not speak out against it.


doing so risked imprisonment or heavy fines.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

today, we see the sedition act as a violation of the first amendment.


the first amendment protects free speech.


despite this, the supreme court upheld the act.


about two thousand people were prosecuted under it.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

one well-known case targeted eugene v. debs.


he was the socialist party's presidential candidate.


debs made comments against the war.


he questioned why working-class men had to lose their lives in a war they had no voice in declaring.


he was sentenced to ten years.


he served more than three.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 7. deportation of dissenters



while debs was imprisoned, others were targeted too.


russian immigrants faced suspicion.


people who supported socialist causes also faced suspicion.


the united states government viewed them as disloyal.


it targeted them for deportation.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

emma goldman is one example.


she was a self-avowed anarchist.


she came to the united states from lithuania as a teenager.


she grew interested in radical politics.


by the time of the war, she was speaking out against the draft.


in 1919, she was deported to russia.


more than two hundred others were deported with her.


this happened under wartime legislation in the united states.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 8. art and poetry as criticism



questioning the war did not always take the form of direct political speech.


some artists used their work to speak out.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

poets published work that showed the cruelty and suffering of the war.


these poets included:


- wilfred owen
- siegfried sassoon
- rupert brooke
- william butler yeats



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

their stanzas did not celebrate heroic charges.


they put the horror on full display.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

visual art also conveyed the misery of the war.


the german artist käthe kollwitz is one example.


she lost her son in the fighting.


her works explored mourning and suffering.


these personal depictions came clearly across to their audiences.


they helped fuel criticism of the war.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 9. bombing and displacement of civilians



the use of aircraft changed the experience of civilians in europe.


for the first time, people at home were in danger.


this was true even where no ground fighting was happening.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

the german military used zeppelins as early as 1915.


they flew to london and bombed the city.


later in the war, germany used gotha bombers to attack the british capital.


hundreds of civilians were killed.


thousands more were injured.


survivors who lost their homes became refugees in their own country.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

the psychological impact was immense.


the role of sheer chance made the danger feel much greater than it was.


a bomb might hit one street and miss the next.


this randomness made every civilian feel at risk.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

# 10. refugees across europe



refugees emerged all over europe.


by the end of 1914, more than three million people in france and belgium alone had left their homes.


many were eventually able to return.


but the problem of housing millions of displaced people continued throughout the war.



&nbsp;

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

on the eastern front, jewish citizens fled their homes by the tens of thousands.


many ended up further east in russia.


anti-semitic laws had previously kept them from going that far.


refugees often fled with very little.


this made their relocations especially painful.

