**a new role for scientists**


&nbsp;


world war two changed science and technology very fast.

these changes soon became matters of **life and death**.


&nbsp;


total war also changed the link between governments and scientists.


&nbsp;


governments poured money into new weapons and tools. they built both deadly and non-deadly technologies. these tools became essential to the war effort and to national security.


&nbsp;


in the fall of 1939, british scientists made a sharp turn.

they moved away from pure research.

instead, they focused on work that would **help win the war**.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


**radar, electronics, and early computers**


&nbsp;


scientists learned more about radio waves. this led to **radar**.

radar gave troops advance warning of enemy attacks.


&nbsp;


other advances brought better electronics into military use. these included microwaves and early digital computing devices.

after the war, these technologies spread into everyday life. they laid the foundation for the commercial electronics we use today.


&nbsp;


allied forces also worked hard to break nazi spy codes. this effort inspired the **first basic computers**.


&nbsp;


**alan turing** was a british mathematician.

he led a team of scientists working for the british secret service. their goal was to crack the **enigma code**. the nazis believed this code was unbreakable.


&nbsp;


turing's team built an electromagnetic machine. it could cycle through hundreds of thousands of possible key and letter combinations in intercepted nazi messages.

in the end, they broke the code.


&nbsp;


another major breakthrough was a computer called **eniac**.

the united states army first developed it to calculate artillery trajectories.


&nbsp;


eniac ran at electronic speed, not mechanical speed.

by 1955, eniac had completed more calculations than all humans had ever solved up to that point.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


**medical advances that saved lives**


&nbsp;


the war also brought life-saving medical progress.

one major development was **penicillin**. scientists improved how penicillin was used and mass-produced it on a large scale.


&nbsp;


another key advance came from **doctor charles drew**, an african american physician.

he developed a process for making blood plasma. this made it easier to store blood for longer periods. plasma also served as an effective replacement for whole-blood transfusions.


&nbsp;


however, racial bias interfered with this work.

in 1941, the american red cross agreed with a war department decision. they began labeling all blood and plasma as either "white" or "negro."


&nbsp;


doctor drew resigned from the national blood bank in protest. he called this policy **racist and unscientific**.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


**the race to build the atomic bomb**


&nbsp;


few developments were more dramatic than the push to build an **atomic bomb**.

this effort mobilized massive scientific and civilian resources.


&nbsp;


in december 1938, two german physicists made a key discovery.

**otto hahn** and **fritz strassmann** accidentally split atoms. they discovered nuclear fission.


&nbsp;


a few months later, the germans set up a secret weapons program called the uranium club. their goal was to create an atomic bomb.


&nbsp;


**albert einstein** was a german-born physicist who had recently moved to the united states. he saw the danger in hahn's work.

physicist **leo szilard** wrote a letter warning president roosevelt about this research. the letter urged the united states to increase its own nuclear efforts. einstein sent it to the president.


&nbsp;


by the late 1930s, british scientists and others were convinced an atomic bomb was possible.

teams of physicists gathered to begin experiments. some of these scientists were refugees from nazi germany. they studied **nuclear chain reactions**, which are the catalysts of an atomic explosion.


&nbsp;


in august 1942, the united states government launched its top-secret **manhattan project**.

the project spread across dozens of sites in the united states. these included:


&nbsp;


los alamos, new mexico


oak ridge, tennessee


hanford, washington state


&nbsp;


a total of **600,000 workers** joined a frenetic race to build the world's first atomic bomb. germany and japan were also trying to build their own bombs.


&nbsp;


the german effort ran into serious problems.

top german scientists had fled the country. some even helped the manhattan project. also, hitler chose to fund v2 bombers for the air war with england instead of an atomic bomb.


&nbsp;


in 1941, japan commissioned physicist **yoshio nishina** to begin work on an atomic bomb. the project was called ni-go.

but it made little progress. japan had no information shared by germany. united states air raids also caused heavy damage and disruption.


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


&nbsp;


**the trinity test**


&nbsp;


in july 1945, the manhattan project finally succeeded.

a bomb was detonated in the **trinity test** at alamogordo, new mexico. this was the first successful trial of an atomic weapon.


&nbsp;


william l. laurence, the official historian of the project, described the moment:


&nbsp;


> "on that moment hung eternity. time stood still. space contracted to a pinpoint. it was as though the earth had opened and the skies split. one felt as though they had been privileged to witness the birth of the world—to be present at the moment of creation when the lord said: let there be light."


&nbsp;


president roosevelt had died suddenly in april 1945.

vice president **harry s. truman** took over as president. it fell to truman to decide whether or not to use the new weapon.

